package cosmonic:agent@0.5.0;

/// The caller may load a toolset into its own workload while it runs. Not
/// published; for discussion.
///
/// Spawning is declarative: a child's toolsets are fixed when it starts. This
/// interface is the separate, explicit power to change a running agent's own
/// toolsets. A world that does not import it cannot load anything, and a
/// spawned child never receives it in this version. A parent cannot load a
/// toolset into a child it spawned; it spawns a new child with different
/// toolsets instead.
///
/// Nothing is special: a toolset is one more component in the caller's own
/// workload, with its own grants, and the caller inherits none of them.
/// Loading is a **rollout**. The implementer admits the request, then rolls
/// the caller's workload out to a new revision that adds the toolset's
/// component, attached to the same session, and retires the old revision.
/// The person does not see the change; the audit log records it. Every
/// component in the new revision must start and pass its checks before the
/// old one retires, so a toolset that cannot start leaves the caller as it
/// was and `load` answers `unavailable`.
///
/// The implementer offers the toolset's definitions through the caller's own
/// `cosmonic:agent/tools` import and dispatches calls to its component, so
/// the caller carries no router; the same implementer must serve that import
/// for loading to work, which is why `tool-loading-provider` exports both.
/// Admission follows `spawn`: the toolset's reach must fit inside the
/// caller's ceiling, and anything beyond it goes to a grantor and returns
/// `needs-approval` with a digest over the caller's identity and the
/// `toolset-spec`. A grant is single use, as for `spawn`: it admits one
/// load in total, even when several identical loads shared the pending
/// approval (see `approval`). Policy may ask for approval even within the
/// ceiling, since new code is new risk even when its reach is not. A
/// `toolset-spec` from a `tool-discovery` offer loads unchanged, and its
/// digest matches what the caller was shown: the digest is computed over the
/// normalized spec in one defined JSON form (see `approval.request-digest`),
/// so the match does not depend on how either side ordered its lists.
///
/// **Already loaded.** Before any ceiling check or grantor, `load` checks
/// the caller's toolsets, in this order:
///
/// 1. A toolset with the same digest (same caller, same normalized spec) is
///    already loaded: `load` returns that `loaded-toolset` unchanged and
///    admits nothing. No approval is raised or consumed and no rollout runs,
///    since the caller already holds exactly this reach.
/// 2. A rollout that adds a toolset with the same digest is in progress: the
///    request joins it and resolves with its result, the same
///    `loaded-toolset` or the same error.
/// 3. Another toolset already holds `name`, whether the caller loaded it or
///    the operator declared it: `invalid-request`, before any approval, so
///    the person is never shown a prompt whose grant could only fail.
///
/// So the waiters that coalesced on one approval all end with the one
/// `loaded-toolset`: whichever resend wins the grant runs the rollout, and
/// the others join it or find it done. A load after `unload` is a new
/// request and is admitted afresh.
///
/// **When the tools arrive.** The new revision serves the caller's next run.
/// The current run is not moved between revisions: it should finish (an
/// agent that loads a tool to use it typically ends the run right after
/// `load` resolves), and the implementer may start the next run on the new
/// revision at once, from the session's journal, so the person sees one
/// continuous turn. Because the caller's instance is replaced, a caller keeps
/// no state in memory across a load; everything it needs is in the session.
///
/// The toolset's tools are offered from the next `tools.list` on, under
/// aliases assigned by the same rules as declared toolsets (own names first,
/// namespaced only on a collision, never renamed). A caller whose `tools`
/// import the implementer does not serve has nowhere to offer them, and
/// `load` answers `unavailable`. An implementer that does not enable loading
/// answers `disabled`.
///
/// The toolset is offered to the component that loaded it and to no sibling
/// component in the same workload, so a sibling cannot use a toolset
/// admitted against another component's ceiling. The same caller identity
/// (workload incarnation and component name, see `spawn-types`) scopes the
/// toolset and binds the approval digest, so a sibling's identical load is a
/// different request: it never coalesces onto, or consumes, this component's
/// approval.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface tool-loading {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{toolset-spec, admission-error};

    /// A toolset the session now carries. A record, not a handle: it lives in
    /// the workload's revision and the session, not in the caller's memory,
    /// so it survives the rollout that loaded it and any later one.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record loaded-toolset {
        /// The toolset's `toolset-spec.name`, unique among the caller's
        /// toolsets. Names the toolset for `unload`.
        name: string,
        /// The aliases under which the caller's `tools` import offers this
        /// toolset's definitions, in the toolset's own order. An alias is the
        /// model-facing selection key and carries no authority. Chosen by the
        /// implementer (see `toolset-spec.alias-prefix`), unique in the
        /// caller's session, and stable for the session: loading another
        /// toolset never changes it.
        aliases: list<string>,
    }

    /// Return the toolset if an identical one is already loaded or loading,
    /// refuse a name already held, and otherwise check the spec against the
    /// ceiling and policy, then roll the caller's workload out to a revision
    /// that adds the toolset, attached to the same session. Resolves once the
    /// new revision is ready to serve the next run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    load: async func(spec: toolset-spec) -> result<loaded-toolset, admission-error>;

    /// Remove a toolset this caller loaded, by name: another rollout, after
    /// which its aliases answer `unknown-tool` but stay reserved for the
    /// session and are never given to another tool. A call in progress is
    /// cancelled cooperatively; its effects are not rolled back. An unknown
    /// name, or a toolset the operator declared rather than one the caller
    /// loaded, answers `invalid-request`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    unload: async func(name: string) -> result<_, admission-error>;

    /// The toolsets this caller loaded and the session still carries, in the
    /// order they were loaded. Declared toolsets are not listed; `tools.list`
    /// shows every tool offered.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    loaded: async func() -> list<loaded-toolset>;
}
